# Testing Policy

## Philosophy
Tests must validate GhostRigger against real game data, not synthetic mocks.
The previous test suite had 5,007 tests but most were synthetic — they tested internal data structures rather than correctness against the actual KotOR game files.

## Test Structure (after cleanup)
tests/
  conftest.py                    — shared fixtures, skip-if-no-game markers
  test_mcp_full_scan.py          — THE primary test: scans ALL 6,078 models via MCP
  test_mcp_texture_validation.py — texture existence and loading for all models
  test_mcp_skinning.py           — skinning correctness for all skin models  
  test_core_contracts.py         — unit tests for vertex_space, render_constants, mdl_reader_wrapper
  test_k2_compat.py              — K2-specific: trimesh header patch, MDX offset 0
  test_regression.py             — regression guards for fixed bugs (Bug C offset, bonemap overflow)

## Rules
- Every test that touches game data MUST use MCP tools or skip gracefully if game not installed
- Use pytest.mark.skipif for missing game installations
- Use pytest parametrize with model lists loaded from JSON (exports/scan_manifest.json)
- No test should take more than 5 seconds per model
- The full scan test can be slow — mark it with @pytest.mark.slow
- Do NOT create tests that mock internal PyKotor structures
- Do NOT create tests that only verify GhostRigger's internal data structures match themselves

## conftest.py Fixtures
- game_k1_available: bool fixture checking K1_PATH exists
- game_k2_available: bool fixture checking K2_PATH exists
- k1_models: list of all K1 MDL resrefs (cached from MCP or manifest)
- k2_models: list of all K2 MDL resrefs (cached from MCP or manifest)
- ghostrigger_tools: importable reference to the five MCP tool functions
